---
title: '261020 : vêtements et couleurs'
tags:
  - fr
---

Q: Conjugate `porter` in the present with `je`.
A: {{`Je porte.`}}
N: Use `porter` to describe the clothes someone is wearing. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “They are wearing boots” using `elles`.
A: {{`Elles portent des bottes.`}}
N: The plural verb ending is `-ent`. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “She is holding an umbrella” using `tenir`.
A: {{`Elle tient un parapluie.`}}
N: `Tenir` specifies that the object is in her hand. Image d. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a sleeveless dress” in French.
A: {{`Une robe sans manches.`}}
N: The dress in image a. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a short-sleeved t-shirt” in French.
A: {{`Un t-shirt à manches courtes.`}}
N: `Manches` is feminine plural. Image b. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a long-sleeved raincoat” in French.
A: {{`Un imperméable à manches longues.`}}
N: Image d. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “striped mittens” using `à rayures`.
A: {{`Des mitaines à rayures.`}}
N: `Rayures` are stripes. Image f. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “patterned boots” using `à motifs`.
A: {{`Des bottes à motifs.`}}
N: `À motifs` describes a repeated pattern more generally. Image e. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “red dresses” in French, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des robes rouges.`}}
N: The simple colour adjective agrees with the feminine plural noun. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “black boots” in French, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des bottes noires.`}}
N: `Noires` agrees with `bottes`, feminine plural. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “orange rain boots” in French, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des bottes de pluie orange.`}}
N: The colour `orange` is invariable. Image d. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “navy-blue blazers” in French, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des vestons bleu marine.`}}
N: A compound colour remains invariable. Image c supplies a singular example. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “light-blue shirts” in French, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des chemises bleu clair.`}}
N: Both words of the compound colour remain invariable. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a brown raincoat” in French.
A: {{`Un imperméable brun.`}}
N: `Brun` is masculine singular. Image d. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “brown boots” using the adjective `brun`, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des bottes brunes.`}}
N: `Brunes` agrees with `bottes`, feminine plural. Image e. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a blue-violet top” in French.
A: {{`Un haut bleu-violet.`}}
N: A broad description of the top in image h. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “flip-flops” in Québec French, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des gougounes.`}}
N: Footwear in image a; familiar Québec vocabulary. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “sneakers” in Québec French, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des espadrilles.`}}
N: Québec usage for this exercise. Image b. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a winter hat” in Québec French.
A: {{`Une tuque.`}}
N: Headwear in image f. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “mittens” in Québec French, including the plural article.
A: {{`Des mitaines.`}}
N: Handwear in image f. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a blazer” in Québec French.
A: {{`Un veston.`}}
N: The jacket in image c. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a swimsuit” in French.
A: {{`Un maillot de bain.`}}
N: The three people in image g wear swimwear. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “a belt” in French.
A: {{`Une ceinture.`}}
N: An accessory visible in image c. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Say “Her dress reaches her knees” using `arriver`.
A: {{`Sa robe arrive aux genoux.`}}
N: `Aux` is the contraction of `à + les`. Image a. [[fr/lessons/261020]]

---

Q: Conjugate `pouvoir` in the present with `je`.
A: {{`Je peux.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 1. `Pouvoir` can express ability, possibility or permission. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `pouvoir` in the present with `tu`.
A: {{`Tu peux.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 1. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `pouvoir` in the present with `il`.
A: {{`Il peut.`}}
N: The same verb form follows `elle` and `on`. Station 7, piste 1. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `pouvoir` in the present with `nous`.
A: {{`Nous pouvons.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 1. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `pouvoir` in the present with `vous`.
A: {{`Vous pouvez.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 1. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `pouvoir` in the present with `ils`.
A: {{`Ils peuvent.`}}
N: The same verb form follows `elles`. Station 7, piste 1. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `devoir` in the present with `je`.
A: {{`Je dois.`}}
N: In these directives, `devoir` expresses obligation. Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `devoir` in the present with `tu`.
A: {{`Tu dois.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `devoir` in the present with `il`.
A: {{`Il doit.`}}
N: The same verb form follows `elle` and `on`. Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `devoir` in the present with `nous`.
A: {{`Nous devons.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `devoir` in the present with `vous`.
A: {{`Vous devez.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Conjugate `devoir` in the present with `ils`.
A: {{`Ils doivent.`}}
N: The same verb form follows `elles`. Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Make `Je peux entrer` negative.
A: {{`Je ne peux pas entrer.`}}
N: Put `ne … pas` around the conjugated modal verb. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Make `Nous pouvons partir` negative.
A: {{`Nous ne pouvons pas partir.`}}
N: `Partir` remains an infinitive. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Make `Ils peuvent entrer` negative.
A: {{`Ils ne peuvent pas entrer.`}}
N: Plural `peuvent` agrees with `ils`. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Give the negative present form of `devoir` with `je`.
A: {{`Je ne dois pas.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Give the negative present form of `devoir` with `vous`.
A: {{`Vous ne devez pas.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Give the negative present form of `devoir` with `ils`.
A: {{`Ils ne doivent pas.`}}
N: Station 7, piste 2. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: What verb form follows a conjugated `pouvoir` or `devoir`?
A: An infinitive.
N: Examples: {{`Tu peux partir.`}} / {{`Nous devons écouter.`}} [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Translate `Nous pouvons partir` into English.
A: We can leave.
N: One of the four printed phrases in station 7, exercise 1. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Which preposition goes before an infinitive after `avoir le droit`?
A: {{`De.`}}
N: {{`Nous avons le droit de parler.`}} = We are allowed to speak. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Replace `nous` with conversational `on`: `Nous pouvons entrer en classe.`
A: {{`On peut entrer en classe.`}}
N: Printed example from station 7, piste 4. `On` takes a third-person singular verb. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Replace `nous` with conversational `on`: `Nous avons le droit de partir.`
A: {{`On a le droit de partir.`}}
N: Printed example from station 7, piste 4. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Replace `nous` with conversational `on`: `Nous devons écouter.`
A: {{`On doit écouter.`}}
N: `On` takes `doit`, third-person singular. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Replace `nous` with conversational `on`: `Nous ne pouvons pas fumer.`
A: {{`On ne peut pas fumer.`}}
N: Keep `ne … pas` around `peut`. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Which grammatical person does the verb take after conversational `on`, even when it means “we”?
A: Third-person singular.
N: {{`On peut.`}} / {{`On doit.`}} / {{`On a.`}} [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Say “You don't have to come” using `être obligé de`, with masculine singular agreement.
A: {{`Tu n'es pas obligé de venir.`}}
N: This explicitly expresses absence of obligation. Feminine: {{`Tu n'es pas obligée de venir.`}} [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]

---

Q: Give the prohibition “You must not smoke” using `tu` and `devoir`.
A: {{`Tu ne dois pas fumer.`}}
N: In a directive, negative `devoir` can express a prohibition. [[fr/lessons/261020#station-7--les-directives-révision-du-jour|Station 7]]
